\textbf{2.1.1 Algebraic and Graphical Languages for Constructing and}
\par
\textbf{Describing Models}
\par
\textbf{To conceptualize and construct a model that adequately des-}
\par
\textbf{cribes a decision situation, an analyst often attempts to visualize}
\par
\textbf{the decision process in both graphical and algebraic terms. For}
\par
\textbf{example, the output of a power generating plant can be described}
\par
\textbf{as an algebraic function of the amount of fuel consumed by the}
\par
\textbf{plant, the energy content of the fuel, and the plant efficiency.}
\par
\textbf{Alternatively, the role of the plant in a complex power generating}
\par
\textbf{and distribution system might be described graphically by a flow}
\par
\textbf{chart that indicates the various material and power flows throughout}
\par
\textbf{the system. Similarly, the probabilistic dependence of interna-}
\par
\textbf{tional events on U.S. foreign policy decisions can be described}
\par
\textbf{algebraically by a set of conditional probability distributions,}
\par
\textbf{or graphically by a decision tree with event probabilities dependent}
\par
\textbf{upon the alternatives chosen by U.S. policy makers.}
\par
\textbf{Some types of problems are easier to conceptualize in alge-}
\par
\textbf{braic terms; other problems, in graphical terms. The choice of a}
\par
\textbf{particular modeling language depends on both the nature of the}
\par
\textbf{problem and the background of the analyst. Some analysts prefer}
\par
\textbf{to visualize complex systems in terms of flow charts, tree struc-}
\par
\textbf{tures, block diagrams, or graphical functions in several dimensions.}
