\par
\textbf{which are outputs. For example, the equation X - X + 1 has no}
\par
\textbf{eolution, but if we define the left side of the equation to be the}
\par
\textbf{output and the right side of the equation to be the input, this}
\par
\textbf{equation defines a perfectly valid operator. This operator has}
\par
\textbf{entity X as both an input and an output; its function is to add}
\par
\textbf{one to the value of X . If we wish to solve algebraic equations}
\par
\textbf{using our definition of entities and operators, it is necessary}
\par
\textbf{to specily the equation in \emph{ftn>> }of an operator with an input and}
\par
\textbf{an output. We can then define a second operator that solves the}
\par
\textbf{equation and produces a third operator with different Inputs and}
\par
\textbf{outputs (see Figure 2.3).}
