\textbf{A slightly more general version of a computational graph Is}
\par
\textbf{a lattice. A lattice Is a tree In which the branches are allowed}
\par
\textbf{to coalesce or connect together. In a lattice there can be}
\par
\textbf{multiple paths between each pair of nodes, but If one of the}
\par
\textbf{nodes Is the origin, then all of the operators along each of the}
\par
\textbf{paths must be oriented In the same direction---either toward the}
\par
\textbf{origin or away from the origin. Figure 2.4b shows an example of}
\par
\textbf{a lattice.}
