\textbf{2'3.1 Sensitivity Analysis}
\par
.
\par
\textbf{One of the basic tools of present-day decision modeling is}
\par
\textbf{sensitivity analysis. By determining the sensitivity of one of the}
\par
\textbf{model parameters to changes in another model parameter, we can gain}
\par
\textbf{some insight into the relative importance of various components of}
\par
\textbf{the model. However, some of our recent research has shown that}
\par
\textbf{sensitivities may be a poor guide in determiuing, for example, the}
\par
\textbf{value of perfect information about an uncertain model parameter.}
\par
\textbf{Furthermore, we currently lack adequate procedures for calculating}
\par
\textbf{and visualizing joint sensitivities: the sensitivity of one model}
\par
\textbf{parameter to changes in several of the other model parameters.}
\par
\textbf{There are a number of ways to measure the sensitivity of one}
\par
\textbf{parameter to another. If both parameters <<re known with certainty,}
\par
\textbf{then we can use a deterministic sensitivity analysis. The most}
\par
\textbf{common way to carry out this form of analysis is to vary one of the}
\par
\textbf{parameters over a specified range, and plot the corresponding values}
\par
\textbf{of the second parameter as a function of the first parameter. The}
\par
\textbf{resulting graph, showing the pairs of pa>>ameter values that fit the}
\par
\textbf{model, describes the global sensitivity of the parameters to each other,}
\par
\textbf{The analysis is global In the sense that the relationship between the}
\par
\textbf{two parameters is described over a given range. On the other hand,}
\par
\textbf{we could compute the local sensitivity of one parameter to another, at}
\par
\textbf{a given operating point, by determining the rate at which small changes}
\par
\textbf{In one parameter cause the other parameter to change. Local sensitiv-}
\par
\textbf{ities are measured in terms of partial derivatives.}
\par
\textbf{Another way to classify sensitivity analyses is by whether they}
\par
\textbf{>> 111}
\par
