\par
\textbf{are }open-loop or closed-loop sensitivities. A closed-loop sensitivity
\par
is one in which the decisions embedded in the model are reoptimized
\par
each time one of the paramters is changed. An or-m-loop sensitivity,
\par
which is usually easier to compute than a closed-loop sensitivity,
\par
is one in which the decisions in the model are fixed throughout the
\par
analysis even though changing one of the parameter values over its
\par
range might change the optimum decision.
\par
For uncertain model parameters, a probabilistic sensitivity
\par
analysis is possible. In its most common form the probabilistic para-
\par
meter is held constant at each of its possible outcome levels and the
\par
resultant effect on the outcome lottery is observed. However, there
\par
are a number of other possibilities. In general, a probabilistic sen-
\par
sitivity involves the modification of the probability distribution for
\par
one model parameter and the observation of the effect on the distribution
\par
for other model paramet=r3. The procedures for probabilistic sensitivity
\par
analysis are not as well defined as those for deterministic sensitivity
\par
analysis because there are a variety of ways in which one can modify
\par
a probability distribution. For example, in one analysis it might be
\par
desirable to vary the variance of one distribution while holding its
\par
mean constant; in another analysis it might be preferable to vary alge-
\par
braic coefficients in the expression for the probability distribution.
\par
As with deterministic sensitivity analyses, probabilistic sensitivity
\par
analyses can be either global or local, and either open-loop or closed-
\par
loop.
