\par
\_
\par
\textbf{.}I' \ding{110} 'I
\par
\textbf{of the decision tree. To do this he needs a procedure for rapidly}
\par
\textbf{selecting a particular location In the decision tree and then looking}
\par
\textbf{at the tree structure surrounding that location, preferably presented}
\par
\textbf{In graphical form. He may also wish to "fly over" the decision tree}
\par
\textbf{following a particular path through the decision tree or moving}
\par
\textbf{accross the decision tree to Investigate similar nodes.}
\par
\textbf{During this process the \textasciicircum m-.yst may find an Incorrect portion}
\par
\textbf{of the decision tree Indicating that the logic he used to construct}
\par
\textbf{the tree was faulty. At this point he might want to make a local change}
\par
\textbf{in the full-scale decision tree. However, since an error in om part}
\par
\textbf{of a large decision tree is often symptomatic of similar errors else-}
\par
\textbf{where in the tree, it would probably be better for the analyst to}
\par
\textbf{return to his simplified representation of the decision tree and}
\par
\textbf{correct the error at that level. Once the error has been corrected}
\par
\textbf{in the simplified version of the decision tree, the entire decision}
\par
\textbf{tree can be regenerated and the analyst can check to see that his}
\par
\textbf{changes have produced the desired results.}
\par
\textbf{A capability that will undoubtedly be needed in future decision}
\par
\textbf{aiding systems is autouiatic processing of probabilities within a}
\par
\textbf{decision tree using Bayes' rule. This form of decision-tree proces-}
\par
\textbf{sing can be Implemented through the use of two decision trees for the}
\par
\textbf{same problem: one decision tree showing the sequence of events and}
\par
\textbf{decisions faced by the decision maker, and another decision tree}
\par
\textbf{(sometimes called a probability assessment tree) that specifies the}
\par
\textbf{same events and decisions in the order in which it is easiest to}
\par
\textbf{assess the necessary probabilities.}
\par
\textbf{If the decision problem is complex, each of these decision trees}
\par
