\textbf{2.4 ELICITATION OF SUBJECTIVE INFORMATION}
\par
\textbf{Automated decision aids have a potentially useful role in the}
\par
\textbf{elicitat ion of subjective information. However, the aids that have}
\par
\textbf{been developed to date in this area have been rudimentary at best.}
\par
\textbf{The primary limitation of current aids is that they are de-}
\par
\textbf{signed to take a probabilistic "snapshot" of a well defined variable.}
\par
\textbf{They record probabilities and values without being sensitive to}
\par
\textbf{biases, conditionality, and shifts in the subject'a opinion. Conse-}
\par
\textbf{quently they never recommend additional training to overcome biases,}
\par
\textbf{or additional structuring or modeling to allow conditioning on}
\par
\textbf{previously unstated variables, or additional encoding to replace data}
\par
\textbf{where the subject has simply changed his mind.}
\par
\textbf{Although automated systems may eventually displace the human}
\par
\textbf{interviewer, initial work in this area will probably be to}
\par
\textbf{develop computerized aids to make the human interviewer more ef-}
\par
\textbf{fective and efficient.}
\par
\textbf{A second problem with current techniques is that they are in-}
\par
\textbf{sensitive to the reason the variable is being encoded. Presumably,}
\par
\textbf{within the context of a given analysis accuracy is more important}
\par
\textbf{for some variables than for others. The order of encoding and re-}
\par
\textbf{sulting conditionalities may affect the value of information cal-}
\par
\textbf{culations that can be performed in an analysis. In other words,}
\par
\textbf{the natural order of developing a model and then encoding the}
\par
\textbf{necessary probabilities applies to the development of automated}
\par
\textbf{aids as well \emph{as to }the execution of a decision analysis project.}
