\textbf{3.2 A GRAPHICAL LANGUAGE FOR MODEL DEVELOPMENT}
\par
\textbf{One contribution of our research effort to develop a mor-}
\par
\textbf{phology for characterizing and analyzing decision problems has}
\par
\textbf{been the development of a graphical-algebraic language for de-}
\par
\textbf{.-ision model specification. The basic elements of this language,}
\par
\textbf{entities and operators, were defin\%d in section 2.1. In that}
\par
\textbf{section we showed how entities and operators could be combined to}
\par
\textbf{represent syster. models. In this section we describe how this}
\par
\textbf{language may serve as the basis for a technique for model develop-}
\par
\textbf{ment. In addition, we Illustrate how this technique might be Im-}
\par
\textbf{plemented as a computerized system for decision analysis.}
\par
\textbf{By far the most difficult and time consuming part of a decision}
\par
\textbf{analysis is the development of a decision model that captures the}
\par
\textbf{Important elements of the problem. Even though the resulting model}
\par
\textbf{may be simple in form, considerable effort is ..Ikely to be re-}
\par
\textbf{quired to choose problem variables and model parameters so as to}
\par
\textbf{represent adequately the essential aspects of the decision.}
\par
\textbf{Increasingly, though, resource allocation problems Involve ex-}
\par
\textbf{tremely complex systems. For example, the choice among pollution}
\par
\textbf{abatement technologies for an electric power plant requires the}
\par
\textbf{understanding of a system whose behavior patterns cannot be dis-}
\par
\textbf{cerned from simplified approximations. Such systems may be quite}
\par
\textbf{large; their bounds are not determined by physical size, but rather}
\par
\textbf{by how much we need to know about the way the system Interacts with}
\par
\textbf{Its environment. For example, for the decision among sulfur oxide}
