\par
\textbf{In Section 2.1 we described a method for concisely specifying}
\par
\textbf{model structure. The method was originally developed to represent}
\par
\textbf{the deterministic dependencies in continuous static models. How-}
\par
\textbf{ever, we shall see in the latter part of this chapter that the}
\par
\textbf{method may be extended to apply to sequential system containing}
\par
\textbf{discrete events as well. Few modeling techniques are sufficiently}
\par
\textbf{general that they can be applied to a broad cross-section of the}
\par
\textbf{model types discussed in Section 1.3.}
\par
\textbf{Any system associated with a resource commitment problem will}
\par
\textbf{contain certain objects of interest. For the system describing}
\par
\textbf{sulfur oxide pollution from an electric power plant, objects of in-}
\par
\textbf{terest include the amount of electricity generated, the quantity}
\par
\textbf{of sulfur oxide emitted from the stacks, and the population downwind}
\par
\textbf{from the plant. Entities will be used to denote objects of interest}
