\textbf{CADMUS may be requested to perform a number of additlouol calcu-}
\par
\textbf{lations which will enable the decision maker to evaluate his al-}
\par
\textbf{ternative decision strategies. For example, he may ask to check}
\par
\textbf{for dominated courses of action; that is, decision strategies which}
\par
\textbf{for all possible state outcomes yield values equaled or exceeded}
\par
\textbf{by other alternative strategies. The decision maker may request}
\par
\textbf{to see the "profit lottery" associated with a given course of}
\par
\textbf{action. CADMUS will then supply the cumulative probability dis-}
\par
\textbf{tribution which will give the probability that the outcome as-}
\par
\textbf{sociated with that decision strategy will have a value less than}
\par
\textbf{or equel to any given amount.}
\par
\textbf{To illustrate the latter possibility suppose that our deci-}
\par
\textbf{sion maker were to specify a probability distribution ror health}
\par
\textbf{cost/lb of sulfur emitted such as that shown in Figure 3.12. A}
\par
\textbf{number of methods to aid the decision maker in assessing such a}
\par
\textbf{distribution are provided by CADMUS. By touching his light pen}
\par
\textbf{to PROB DIST}
\par
\textbf{("probability distribution" control button) and}
\par
\textbf{then to (TOTAL ANNUAL SOCIETAL COST\textasciicircum{} , the decision maker would}
\par
\textbf{instruct CADMUS to produce cumulative probability distributions}
\par
\textbf{for "total annual societal cost" under the various decision alter-}
\par
\textbf{natives (Figure 3.13). The curve associated with a given decision}
\par
\textbf{strategy would show, for that strategy, the probability that total}
\par
\textbf{annual societal cost lay below any given amount.}
