\par
\textbf{Structure Approach to Single Stage Decision Models.}
\par
\textbf{\textasciicircum{} In our preceding exercise the postulated computerized system for de-}
\par
\textbf{cision analysis was employed primarily to Illustrate the usefulness of the}
\par
\textbf{branching structure language of entitles and operators for representing}
\par
\textbf{x single stage deterministic models. The branching structure language pro-}
\par
\textbf{vides a convenient method to record a well-specified deterministic value}
\par
\textbf{model. The It guage Is also a tool to aid In model construction. Using}
\par
\textbf{the "crystal ball" algorithm a user can develop a model right at the com-}
\par
\textbf{puter terminal.}
\par
\textbf{3'3 APPLICATION OF THE GRAPHICAL LANGUAGE FOR MODEL DEVELOPMENT TO A}
\par
\textbf{SEQUENTIAL PROBABILISTIC DECISTON PROBLEM WITH A DISCRETE EVENT}
\par
\textbf{STRUCTURE.}
\par
\textbf{The concept of using a branching structure as a modallng language}
\par
\textbf{was originally developed to apply to deterministic, single stage con-}
\par
\textbf{tinuous variable models. At the end of the previous section we showed}
\par
\textbf{that relaxation of the deterministic restriction is not difficult. Basic-}
\par
\textbf{ally, by holding the decision variables at a specific setting and sptcify-}
\par
\textbf{ing probability distributions on the state variables it is straight-}
\par
\textbf{forward to generate a "profit lottery," a probability distribution on}
\par
\textbf{j the output. Surprisingly, it is also possible to relax the other two con-}
\par
\textbf{straint i and consider sequential decisions and discrete variables within}
\par
\textbf{the same general branching structure.}
\par
\textbf{To illustrate, we shall use a sample problem called the Used Car Buyer,}
\par
