\textbf{For the Used Car Buyer Problem, probability assessment Is}
\par
\textbf{most natural in the order represented in Figure 3.23. Joe believes}
\par
\textbf{that there is a one in five chance that the used car he is interested}
\par
\textbf{in is a lemon. So, the a priori probability of the car being a}
\par
\textbf{lemon is 0.2, and the a priori probability of it being a peach is}
\par
\textbf{0.8. Joe must now determine the probabilities of the various pos-}
\par
\textbf{sibl.>> test outcomes given that the car is a lemon or given that}
\par
\textbf{it is a peach. This is a simple problem in combinatorial mathe-}
\par
\textbf{matics.}
\par
\textbf{In a lemon exactly six of the csr's ten systems will be de-}
\par
\textbf{fective. Suppose that one major system of the car is tested. Since}
\par
\textbf{the car is a lemon, there is probability 6 in 10, or 0.6, that the}
\par
\textbf{system being tested happens to be one of the defective systems.}
\par
\textbf{Therefore, if the car is a lemon and the "test type" Is "test 1",}
\par
\textbf{then there is probability 0.6 that the test outcome will be "defect"}
\par
\textbf{and a probability 0.4 that this outcome will be "no defect".}
\par
\textbf{/}
\par
\textbf{Suppose that a defective system is found on the first test.}
\par
\textbf{There are nine remaining untested systems in the car, and, out of}
\par
\textbf{those nine, five are defective. Therefore, if a second system is}
\par
\textbf{tested there is probability 5 In 9, or 5/9, that this system will}
\par
\textbf{likewise be defective. Hence, the probability of finding two de-}
\par
\textbf{fective systems in two tests is 0.6 x 5/9 - 1/3. Stated differently,}
\par
\textbf{if the oar is a lemon, and if the "test type" is "test 2", then}
\par
\textbf{there is probability 1/3 that the test outcome is "two defects".}
